# Limitations, and how to explain them

Say them before you are asked. For each: what it is, why it does not invalidate the result, and what we did about it.

1. **FlavorDB has changed since 2018.** The 2018 snapshot used by Singh & Bagler is offline, so we use today's FlavorDB. *How to explain:* "We replicated before extending: {{ R.random.sign_agreement }}/{{ R.random.n }} signs and Spearman ρ = {{ f(R.random.spearman) }} with the published Z. Contrasting cuisines come out somewhat more contrasting (Korea {{ f(R.korea_z, 1) }} vs {{ R.korea_z_published }}), which in effect-size terms is small because Z = √N_rand × d."
2. **LLM cuisines are small ({{ F.design.recipes_per_region }} recipes per region).** *How to explain:* "That is exactly why we built the equal-size reference: genuine samples of that size share the full cuisine's sign only {{ pct(E.genuine_sign_agreement, 0) }} of the time, so every LLM verdict is against genuine samples of the same size, with BH correction."
3. **The model roster is not complete.** {% for k in F.incomplete_models %}{{ F.models[k].name }}: {{ F.models[k].status }}{{ "; " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} *How to explain:* "These are stated as status; nothing is extrapolated. The proposal's minimum (≥ 10,000 recipes, 1 proprietary + 2 open families) is met with {{ n0(ds.total_recipes) }} recipes."
4. **Aliasing of LLM text is not yet curated.** {{ pctr([ds.alias_full_min, ds.alias_full_max]) }} of LLM lines are fully matched; unmatched terms such as "gochujang" wait for human review. *How to explain:* "The aliaser is CulinaryDB's own protocol, validated at {{ pct(F.aliasing.acc_lines) }} on held-out CulinaryDB lines; curation can only add matches, it does not change the protocol."
5. **Steering ran at a smaller scale than pre-registered.** {% if B %}Best-of-N reranked the {{ B.meta.pool_max }} existing recipes per region instead of a {{ n0(SD.pool_target) }}-candidate pool, with {{ B.meta.selected_max }} picks per N.{% endif %} *How to explain:* "The full pool would cost about {{ f(SD.gpu_hours_min, 0) }}–{{ f(SD.gpu_hours_max, 0) }} GPU-hours per model on the free Kaggle T4 (measured from our runs). We documented the deviation; at this size cuisine-level metrics are noisy, so we read best-of-N as a trade-off and critique-and-revise as promising, not proven."
6. **The evaluation classifier is still an ingredient classifier.** *How to explain:* "It is a different model class on a disjoint split, but both learn ingredients → cuisine, so we also report metrics the scorer cannot game: prevalence, fingerprints, pairing sign, diversity."
7. **No human taste test.** *How to explain:* "Fidelity is measured against CulinaryDB's web recipes; a blind rating by cooks (a "Turing test for chefs") is the natural next step."
